export const LINKEDIN_POST_BODIES: Record<string, string> = {"233-resources-110-concepts-100-experts-one-free-ai-learning-system":"<ul><li><p>23 learning topics </p></li><li><p>110 AI concepts 100 expert profiles </p></li><li><p>90+ curated videos </p></li><li><p>90+ courses and sites </p></li><li><p>70+ GitHub repos </p></li><li><p>60+ books and papers </p></li><li><p>30+ data fields per expert </p></li><li><p>10 concept categories </p></li><li><p>6 learning phases </p></li><li><p>4 practice milestones, 18 weeks start to finish</p></li></ul><p>I built a complete AI learning system and published it as a website. Free. No login. No email capture. No upsell. Note, happy to hear your recommendation</p><ul><li><p><strong>Roadmap: </strong><a href=\"/notebook/ai/roadmap\"><strong>https://venkatapagadala.com/notebook/ai/roadmap</strong></a></p></li><li><p><strong>Concepts Encyclopedia : </strong><a href=\"/notebook/ai/encyclopedia\"><strong>https://venkatapagadala.com/notebook/ai/encyclopedia</strong></a></p></li><li><p><strong>Top 100 Contributors:</strong> <a href=\"/notebook/ai\"><strong>https://venkatapagadala.com/notebook/ai</strong></a></p></li></ul><p>Here's what's inside.</p><p><strong>The Roadmap - 23 topics, 90+ videos, 90+ courses, 70+ repos</strong></p><figure><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0506.webp\" alt=\"Venkata's site: AI Learning Roadmap with phase filters\" width=\"2232\" height=\"1421\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0506\"></figure><p><strong>Roadmap: </strong><a href=\"/notebook/ai/roadmap\"><strong>https://venkatapagadala.com/notebook/ai/roadmap</strong></a></p><ul><li><p>6 phases. 18 weeks. Zero to building LLM applications.</p></li><li><p>🟢 Foundations → 🔵 Python &amp; Math → 🟡 Machine Learning → 🟠 Deep Learning → 🔴 NLP &amp; LLMs → 🟣 Specialize &amp; Build</p></li><li><p>Every topic: 3-4 videos, 3-4 courses, 3-4 GitHub repos, 2-3 books, recommended tools.</p></li><li><p>Videos from 3Blue1Brown, Andrej Karpathy, Andrew Ng, StatQuest, freeCodeCamp, Stanford CS229, CS231n, CS224n, MIT 6.S191.</p></li><li><p>Courses from fast.ai, Coursera, Kaggle, Hugging Face, Khan Academy, Google, DeepLearning.AI.</p></li><li><p>Repos: nanoGPT (38K stars)</p></li><li><p>scikit-learn (60K), huggingface/transformers (140K), LangChain (100K), d2l-en (55K), stable-diffusion-webui (140K).</p></li><li><p>4 practice milestones built into the sequence. Not suggestions - checkpoints. Build an ML project. Build a deep learning project. Build an LLM application. Build a portfolio.</p></li><li><p>Topics most roadmaps skip: prompt engineering, AI safety and ethics, data engineering, model evaluation, AI agents, and reading research papers.</p></li></ul><p><strong>The Encyclopedia - 110 concepts, 10 categories, 3 difficulty levels</strong></p><figure><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0508.webp\" alt=\"Venkata's site: AI Concepts Encyclopedia page\" width=\"2232\" height=\"1401\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0508\"></figure><p>Every concept from \"What is Data?\" to State Space Models.</p><p><strong>🧠 Concepts Encyclopedia : </strong><a href=\"/notebook/ai/encyclopedia\"><strong>https://venkatapagadala.com/notebook/ai/encyclopedia</strong></a></p><p>Each entry: description in plain English, key terms, prerequisites, real-world applications, best free resource link.</p><ul><li><p>🏗️ Foundations - 15 concepts</p></li><li><p>📊 Machine Learning - 20 concepts </p></li><li><p>🧠 Deep Learning - 20 concepts </p></li><li><p>💬 NLP &amp; Language Models - 17 concepts </p></li><li><p>👁️ Computer Vision - 8 concepts </p></li><li><p>🎮 Reinforcement Learning - 8 concepts </p></li><li><p>🎨 Generative AI - 8 concepts </p></li><li><p>⚙️ MLOps &amp; Infrastructure - 7 concepts </p></li><li><p>🛡️ AI Safety &amp; Ethics - 4 concepts </p></li><li><p>🔬 Advanced Research - 3 concepts</p></li></ul><p>Prerequisite chains connect every concept. You always know what comes next.</p><p><strong>The People - 100 experts, 30+ fields each, every link clickable</strong></p><p>Geoffrey Hinton. Yoshua Bengio. Yann LeCun. Andrej Karpathy. Andrew Ng. Demis Hassabis. Ilya Sutskever. Fei-Fei Li. Ian Goodfellow. Sam Altman. Dario Amodei. And 89 others.</p><figure><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0507.webp\" alt=\"Geoffrey Hinton contributor profile page (variant of 0505)\" width=\"2232\" height=\"1439\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0507\"></figure><p><strong>📚 Top 100 Contributors:</strong> <a href=\"/notebook/ai\">https://venkatapagadala.com/notebook/ai</a> </p><p><strong>Every profile:</strong></p><ul><li><p>Bio. </p></li><li><p>Affiliation. </p></li><li><p>Country. </p></li><li><p>Specialty. </p></li><li><p>AI segment. </p></li><li><p>Expert type. Website. Twitter/X. GitHub. LinkedIn. YouTube. Google Scholar. Substack. </p></li><li><p>Education. PhD advisor. Awards and recognition. </p></li><li><p>Citations. Company valuation. </p></li><li><p>Books. Notable talks. </p></li><li><p>Podcasts with clickable links. </p></li><li><p>Interviews with clickable links. </p></li><li><p>Top projects. Top papers. GitHub followers. </p></li><li><p>GitHub pinned repos. Board memberships. </p></li><li><p>Key influence.</p></li></ul><p>15 AI segments: Foundation Models &amp; LLMs, Computer Vision, Robotics, AI Safety, NLP, Generative AI, Reinforcement Learning, ML Theory, Open Source AI, AI Hardware, AI for Science, AI Education, Enterprise AI, AI Ethics, AI Agents.</p><p>5 expert types: Researcher, Industry Leader, Founder/CEO, Educator, Policy Advocate.Global coverage: USA, Canada, UK, China, France, Germany, Israel, Switzerland, India, South Korea, and more.</p>","i-classified-5-3-million-keywords-in-under-a-second":"<p>Last month, I posted about a system I built: a Topical Clustering Engine that structures 5.3 million raw search queries into a 5-level taxonomy - Categories, Subcategories, Intents, Topics, and Keywords  at under 1 millisecond per classification.</p><p>The post got 254 reactions, 89 comments, and 11 reposts.</p><p><strong>Venkata Pagadala</strong> AI Product Manager | 10+ Years in Search &amp; SEO Building AI systems that actually work at enterprise scale.</p><p>🌐 <a href=\"/\"><strong>venkatapagadala.com</strong></a> - Deep dives, tools, and resources 🔗 [Follow me on LinkedIn for weekly insights between editions →]</p><p>But the most common question wasn't about the technology. It was this:</p><p><strong>\"How did you actually build this?\"</strong></p><p>So let me tell you the story.</p><h3>The Problem Nobody Talks About</h3><p>Every large enterprise I've worked at has the same invisible problem: they're drowning in search data but starving for search intelligence.</p><p>Here's what I mean.</p><p>Imagine a company that operates over 40 million web pages. Millions of people search for things related to their products every single day. And like every large enterprise, they have keyword data. Spreadsheets of it. Dashboards of it. Terabytes of it.</p><p>But when leadership asked a simple question - <em>\"What are ALL the topics our customers care about?\"</em>  nobody could answer it.</p><p>Not because the data didn't exist. Because nobody could organize it at scale.</p><p>A skilled SEO analyst can manually categorize about 1,000 keywords in 2 hours. At that rate, organizing 5.3 million keywords would take approximately 10,600 hours. That's 5 years of full-time work. For one snapshot. That's already outdated by the time you finish.</p><p>This is the dirty secret of the search industry: we have more data than ever and less understanding than ever.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:ugcPost:7421367059529220096/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Embedded LinkedIn post: view it on LinkedIn</a></p><h3>Why Traditional AI Approaches Failed</h3><p>My first instinct was to throw an LLM at it. GPT-4, Claude - just feed in the keywords and ask it to categorize them.</p><p>It didn't work. Here's why:</p><p><strong>Cost.</strong> Classifying 5.3M keywords through GPT-4 API calls would cost thousands of dollars per run. And you need to run it regularly because search behavior changes.</p><p><strong>Speed.</strong> Even with batching, LLM-based classification took hours. Business decisions can't wait hours for a taxonomy to rebuild.</p><p><strong>Consistency.</strong> LLMs are creative, which is exactly what you don't want in classification. Ask Claude to categorize \"iPhone 16 Pro Max case\" ten times and you might get ten slightly different category paths.</p><p><strong>Hallucination.</strong> LLMs would sometimes invent categories that didn't exist in our taxonomy, or merge categories that should stay separate.</p><p>I needed something different. Something that combined AI intelligence with engineering precision.</p><h3>The Architecture That Actually Works</h3><p>What I built is a hybrid system - not a single model, but a pipeline:</p><p><strong>Layer 1: Taxonomy Design (Human + AI)</strong> I worked with domain experts to define the initial category structure. This is the one step where human judgment is irreplaceable. You need to know what \"Devices,\" \"Internet,\" \"Accessories,\" and \"Support\" mean in your business context. AI helped expand and refine, but humans set the foundation.</p><p><strong>Layer 2: Training Data Generation (AI-Assisted)</strong> Using a combination of LLMs and rule-based systems, I generated high-quality training examples for each category at every level of the taxonomy. The key insight: use LLMs for training data generation (where creativity is an asset), not for real-time classification (where consistency is everything).</p><p><strong>Layer 3: Classification Engine (Purpose-Built ML)</strong> The actual classifier is a fine-tuned model optimized for one thing: speed and accuracy at scale. No API calls. No token costs. Sub-millisecond inference. It runs locally and processes millions of keywords in seconds.</p><p><strong>Layer 4: Quality Assurance (Automated)</strong> A separate validation layer checks for edge cases, ambiguous classifications, and category drift over time. This is where I use LLMs again - as a quality auditor, not as the primary classifier.</p><p>The result:</p><ul><li><p><strong>5,331,768 keywords classified</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>913 L1 categories</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>746 L2 subcategories</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>882 L3 intents</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>&lt;1ms classification speed</strong></p></li></ul><p>What used to take a team 5 years now takes a machine under a minute.</p><h3>The Lesson That Changes Everything</h3><p>Here's what building this system taught me - and it's the core thesis of this newsletter:</p><p><strong>The companies winning with AI aren't using AI to replace thinking. They're using AI to make thinking possible at a scale that was previously impossible.</strong></p><p>Before this system, nobody at a company managing tens of millions of pages could answer \"what do our customers actually care about?\" Not because they were lazy. Because it was humanly impossible.</p><p>Now we can see the entire landscape. 1.6 million topics. 789 categories. Every keyword mapped to an intent, every intent mapped to a business opportunity.</p><p>That's not automation. That's augmentation. And it's a fundamentally different philosophy than \"let AI write your blog posts.\"</p><h3>What's Coming Next</h3><p>This is Edition #1 of Signal Over Noise. Here's what I'm building toward:</p><p><strong>Edition #2:</strong> How Netflix, Amazon, and Google architect their knowledge graphs - and what every company can learn from their approaches.</p><p><strong>Edition #3:</strong> From taxonomy to strategy - how to turn 5 million classified keywords into a 3-4 year content and optimization roadmap.</p><p><strong>Edition #4:</strong> The internal linking recommendation engine - using AI to connect content across millions of pages.</p><p>Every edition will follow the same principle: less hype, more signal. Real systems. Real architectures. Real results.</p><hr><h3>Your Turn</h3><p>I built this system for a Fortune 500 company, but the architecture works for any industry with scale.</p><p><strong>Drop a comment below: What industry are you in, and what would you do with a complete map of every topic your customers search for?</strong></p><p>I'll pick the most interesting industry from the comments and show how this system would work for it in a future edition.</p><p><em>If this was useful, share it with someone who's tired of AI hype and wants to see what AI actually looks like in production.</em></p><p><em>And if you're new here - welcome to Signal Over Noise. Subscribe to get the next edition delivered to your inbox. For more resources and deep dives, visit </em><a href=\"/\"><em>venkatapagadala.com</em></a><em>.</em></p>","improving-seo-with-conceptual-models-mark-williams-cook":"<p>Here are the top 10 important points from <strong>Improving your SEO with conceptual models - </strong><a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/feed/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><strong>Mark Williams-Cook</strong></a></p><p><strong>Video: </strong><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_AQ9UDqES80\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_AQ9UDqES80</a> </p><p><strong>Image Credits:</strong> <a href=\"http://searchnorwich.org\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">searchnorwich.org</a></p><h3>Topics Discussed</h3><p>Conceptual Models for SEO</p><p>Google's Optimization Goal</p><p>Algorithm Updates and the Meta</p><p>Site Quality Score (I loved this one - Talks about how brand matters for SEO) </p><p>AI Content and the Site Quality Problem</p><p>Google Predicts Click Probability</p><p>Impact of the Helpful Content Update</p><p>SEO Game Strategy</p><h2>Conceptual Models for SEO</h2><p>Advanced SEO is about building conceptual models - stitching together knowledge of how search works to understand what drives rankings, rather than focusing solely on individual tactics.</p><h2>Google's Optimization Goal</h2><p>While Google tells SEOs to create \"helpful content,\" it's real goal balances relevance with its need to make vast amounts of money. The resulting algorithm reflects this dual target.</p><h2>Algorithm Updates and the Meta</h2><p>Google’s updates are like a “meta” in gaming: SEOs identify what works (the meta), exploit it, and Google changes the rules to realign results. This cycle repeats continuously.</p><figure><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0274.webp\" alt=\"SearchNorwich talk frame: speaker beside a complex data-flow diagram\" width=\"1907\" height=\"992\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0274\"></figure><h2>Site Quality Score</h2><p>Google uses a Site Quality Score (0 to 1) at the subdomain level to determine rankings and eligibility for features like rich snippets. <strong>This score combines brand signals like branded search volume, anchor text mentions, and CTR.</strong></p><figure><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0276.webp\" alt=\"SearchNorwich slide: bar chart of subdomains by site_quality score\" width=\"1232\" height=\"1071\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0276\"></figure><h2>Consensus and Query Classifiers</h2><p>Google calculates a consensus score for content (agree, contradict, or neutral) to determine reliability. Specific query classifiers like “is debunking” or “YM YL” (Your Money, Your Life) trigger stricter algorithms.</p><figure><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0277.webp\" alt=\"SearchNorwich slide: frequency chart of semantic query classes\" width=\"1132\" height=\"1005\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0277\"></figure><h2>Ranking vs Post-Ranking Adjustments</h2><p>There’s a distinction between “ranking” and “post-ranking”: Google doesn’t directly use CTR in initial ranking, but post-ranking adjustments consider how users interact with SERPs to refine results.</p><figure><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0280.webp\" alt=\"SearchNorwich slide: Sistrix visibility for Causal.app AI content spike and drop\" width=\"1218\" height=\"1004\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0280\"></figure><h2>AI Content and the Site Quality Problem</h2><p>AI content caused Google's site quality prediction model to fail, as AI-generated content often initially scores high. This led to the Helpful Content Update, which now heavily favors authoritative and well-known brands.</p><figure><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0281.webp\" alt=\"SearchNorwich slide: high SEO metrics, low brand metrics punished by HCU\" width=\"1858\" height=\"1013\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0281\"></figure><h2>Google Predicts Click Probability</h2><p>Google has a model that predicts click-through rates for search results, based on factors like title tags. While CTR isn’t a ranking factor, performing better than predicted impacts visibility.</p><figure><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0275.webp\" alt=\"SearchNorwich slide: JSON with click_age_probability and site_quality fields\" width=\"1839\" height=\"1038\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0275\"></figure><figure><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0279.webp\" alt=\"SearchNorwich talk frame: speaker with domain site-quality score list\" width=\"1871\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0279\"></figure><h2>Impact of the Helpful Content Update</h2><p>The update penalized sites with high domain authority (SEO-focused) but low brand authority (unrecognized brands), reinforcing Google's focus on trust and brand recognition.</p><figure><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0278.webp\" alt=\"SearchNorwich slide: &quot;There is a Site quality score patent&quot;\" width=\"1204\" height=\"1002\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0278\"></figure><h2>SEO Game Strategy</h2><p>Like optimizing games such as Theme Park, SEOs should balance “documented” (title tags, content) and “undocumented” (user signals, brand metrics) strategies to align with Google’s ever-changing meta.</p><p>These points summarize the core themes around advanced SEO strategies, algorithm behavior, and Google's priorities.</p>","brighton-seo-conference-2024":"<h3>3 Reasons why you need to attend </h3><ul><li><p>Its highly impossible to meet 100+ SEO's in one place </p></li><li><p>Build network, find mentors, meaningful relations. Learn more about tools, technologies ( AI &amp; ML ) and different use cases </p></li><li><p>Best investment is Invent yourself </p></li></ul><p>Check out <a href=\"https://us.brightonseo.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Brighton SEO Conference on</a> <a href=\"https://us.brightonseo.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><strong>23 &amp; 24 Sep, 2025 at </strong>Manchester Grand Hyatt San Diego, CA, USA</a> </p><p>Note: This is not affiliated link AKA (I don't earn anything from ticket purchases) </p><h2>Who is Venkata Pagadala ?</h2><p>Venkata Pagadala is an SEO and Growth Strategist who helps businesses achieve online success through data-driven tactics. Worked on campaigns that generate over 40+ million organic search visits per month, worked with industry leaders like <a href=\"https://Apartments.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apartments.com</a>, <a href=\"https://americanaddictioncenters.org\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">americanaddictioncenters.org</a>, Rehabs.com, and over 100+ clients globally ( Local and International).  Leveraging AI and Machine Learning, he excels in content generation and complex site migrations.</p><p>Currently learning about <a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/posts/venkata-pagadala_seo-ai-machinelearning-activity-7260789112796860416-t114\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RAG</a>, Multimodal Retrieval-Augmented Generation Ai Agents AND building by site: <a href=\"/\">venkatapagadala.com</a> </p><p>Had a pleasure to meet alot of people from SEO community: <a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/schwartze/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Eli Schwartz</a> , <a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/samantha-torres-seo/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Samantha Torres,</a> <a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/volpini/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Andrea (cyberandy)Volpini,</a> <a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/tysonstockton/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Tyson Stockton,</a> <a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/noahlearner/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Noah learner</a>, ( Thanks for Boardwalk Bash ), <a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/singhalanurag/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Anurag Singhal,</a> <a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/jonmuranko/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Jon Muranko,</a> <a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/paulandre/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Paul Andre (DRE) de Vera.</a> Check out SEO Video Show, <a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/patrickstox/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Patrick Stox</a></p><p>Missed meeting with <a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/nickleroyseo/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Nick LeRoy .</a> Check Out: <a href=\"https://www.seojobs.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">https://www.seojobs.com/</a>, <a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/wilreynolds/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wil reynolds</a></p><h2>Brighton SEO - San Diego 2024 - Table WIP</h2><p>Note: I am working on table  | Speaker | Topic | Slides | Connect aka LinkedIn | Company |)</p><ul><li><p><a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1Em3ZttYjUqokJw0sByKZyAaUXmhdOMT3mmdTC-ESTAw/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1Em3ZttYjUqokJw0sByKZyAaUXmhdOMT3mmdTC-ESTAw/edit?usp=sharing</a></p></li></ul><p>Note: I will update the article soon. This is a big event, so hard to do everything in once and any contribution are most welcomed ( Notes, Videos or Audios are super helpful )</p><h2>Rand Fishkin  from SparkToro, </h2><p><a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/randfishkin/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">https://www.linkedin.com/in/randfishkin/</a></p><p>I learned a lot from this Him &amp; Absolutely Fan Moment and thank <a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/parthsuba77/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">parth suba</a> for contributions<a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/parthsuba77?miniProfileUrn=urn%3Ali%3Afsd_profile%3AACoAAA9DLP4BkifTgbCS40o9O1Jhn6hXjOH1vpQ\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">.</a></p><figure><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0263.webp\" alt=\"Selfie with a man in a striped sweater (similar to 0253)\" width=\"1124\" height=\"1500\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0263\"></figure><p><strong>Understanding the Shifts in Digital Marketing: Insights from Rand Fishkin at Brighton SEO San Diego 2024</strong></p><p>Attended an great session by <strong>Rand Fishkin</strong>, co-founder of SparkToro, at BrightonSEO San Diego 2024. Rand delved deep into the current state of digital marketing, challenging common perceptions and presenting data-driven theories on why marketing has become increasingly challenging.</p><hr><h3>Key Theories and Findings:</h3><h3>1. COVID-19 Accelerated Digital Growth Temporarily</h3><ul><li><p><strong>Theory:</strong> The pandemic shifted anticipated organic growth in digital engagement to 2020-2021, causing a plateau afterward.</p></li><li><p><strong>Data Support: </strong>Worldwide internet growth spiked during the pandemic but slowed down subsequently.</p></li><li><p><strong>Internet Adoption:</strong> Saturation in wealthy countries, with future growth expected primarily in Asia and Africa.</p></li><li><p><strong>Time Spent Online:</strong> Reached maximum levels; people have limited additional time to spend online.</p></li></ul><h3>2. Economic Factors Aren't Hindering Marketing Success</h3><ul><li><p><strong>Theory:</strong> Contrary to popular belief, the economy isn't the primary reason marketing feels harder.</p></li><li><p><strong>Data Refutation: Consumer Spending:</strong> Remains robust in both B2B and consumer sectors.</p></li><li><p><strong>GDP Growth and Unemployment:</strong> Healthy GDP growth and historically low unemployment rates.</p></li><li><p><strong>Wages vs. Inflation:</strong> Wages have outpaced inflation in recent years.</p></li><li><p><strong>Bankruptcy Filings:</strong> At lower levels compared to previous years.</p></li><li><p><strong>Global Advertising Spend:</strong> Continues to grow, indicating sustained investment in marketing.</p></li></ul><h3>3. Social Media Reach and Engagement are Declining</h3><ul><li><p><strong>Theory:</strong> Organic reach and engagement on major social platforms are decreasing.</p></li><li><p><strong>Evidence: Declining Engagement Rates:</strong> Median reach per post is dismal across platforms like Facebook, Instagram, and Twitter.</p></li><li><p><strong>Zero-Sum Growth:</strong> Growth of platforms like TikTok and Reddit comes at the expense of others. <strong>New Platforms:</strong> Emerging platforms like Threads and Bluesky are growing but require attention to gain traction.</p></li><li><p><strong>Exception:</strong> <strong>Email Marketing Consistency Over Time:</strong> Open and click-through rates for email have remained stable since 2005.</p></li><li><p><strong>Reliability:</strong> Email continues to be a dependable channel for reaching audiences.</p></li></ul><h3>4. AI and Large Language Models Aren't Replacing Google Search</h3><ul><li><p><strong>Theory:</strong> Despite the growth of AI platforms like ChatGPT, they aren't significantly cannibalizing Google Search's market share.</p></li><li><p><strong>Data Insights:Usage Comparison:</strong> ChatGPT's search volume is a fraction of Google's.</p></li><li><p><strong>Google Search Stability:</strong> Searches per user on Google remain steady or have increased.</p></li><li><p><strong>Traffic Referral:</strong> The majority of web traffic still originates from Google Search.</p></li></ul><h3>5. The Dominance of Zero-Click Searches on Google</h3><ul><li><p><strong>Theory:</strong> A significant portion of Google searches result in no clicks to external websites.</p></li><li><p><strong>Data Highlights: Zero-Click Searches:</strong> Approximately 37% of searches result in no click.</p></li><li><p><strong>Clicks to Websites:</strong> Only about 36% of searches lead to clicks on non-Google-owned websites.</p></li><li><p><strong>Implications:</strong> Marketers face challenges in driving traffic from search results due to Google's ecosystem retaining users.</p></li></ul><h3>6. Building on 'Rented Land' Is Becoming Necessary</h3><ul><li><p><strong>Theory:</strong> Marketers should consider engaging audiences directly on platforms they don't own (e.g., social media) due to diminishing organic reach elsewhere.</p></li><li><p><strong>Rationale:Influence Over Traffic:</strong> Emphasizes the importance of brand influence even without direct website traffic.</p></li><li><p><strong>Content Consumption:</strong> Users spend significant time on social platforms, consuming content without necessarily clicking through.</p></li></ul><h3>7. Evolving Buyer Journeys</h3><ul><li><p><strong>Theory:</strong> Traditional buyer journeys have shifted; consumers now often consume content and make decisions without direct website interactions.</p></li><li><p><strong>Data Support:</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Search Intent Analysis:Navigational Searches:</strong> Constitute about 32% of searches.<strong>Informational Searches:</strong> Make up roughly 50%.</p></li><li><p><strong>Commercial and Transactional Searches:</strong> Only about 15%.</p></li><li><p><strong>Topic Distribution:</strong> Entertainment and informational content dominate search queries. </p></li><li><p><strong>Long-Tail Keywords:</strong> A vast number of unique, low-volume searches indicate diverse user interests.</p></li></ul><hr><h3>Actionable Takeaways:</h3><h3>Diversify Beyond Traffic Acquisition:</h3><ul><li><p><strong>Embrace Multiple Channels:</strong> Relying solely on search traffic is limiting; explore social media, email, and other platforms for audience engagement.</p></li><li><p><strong>Focus on Influence:</strong> Prioritize brand influence and awareness over direct traffic metrics.</p></li></ul><h2>Reevaluate KPIs:</h2><ul><li><p><strong>Move Beyond Traffic Metrics:</strong> Understand that traffic does not directly correlate with revenue or influence.</p></li><li><p><strong>Adopt Non-Traffic KPIs:</strong> Measure success through engagement, brand searches, and social visibility.</p></li></ul><h3>Leverage Zero-Click Content:</h3><ul><li><p><strong>Native Platform Engagement:</strong> Create content optimized for in-platform consumption on social media to maximize reach.</p></li><li><p><strong>Avoid Over-Reliance on Links:</strong> Posts without external links often achieve higher engagement rates.</p></li></ul><h3>Understand Your Audience's Attention Habits:</h3><ul><li><p><strong>Conduct Audience Research:</strong> Identify where your target audience spends time and tailor content for those platforms.</p></li><li><p><strong>Use Tools for Insights:</strong> Utilize platforms like SparkToro or SimilarWeb to gain insights into audience behaviors.</p></li></ul><h3>Build Correlation Dashboards:</h3><ul><li><p><strong>Track Multi-Channel Impact:</strong> Correlate social engagement, brand mentions, and other metrics with conversions and revenue.</p></li><li><p><strong>Data-Driven Decisions:</strong> Use insights to inform strategy and demonstrate value to stakeholders.</p></li></ul><hr><h3>Final Thoughts</h3><p>Rand Fishkin highlighted the necessity for marketers to adapt to the evolving digital landscape. With the saturation of internet usage and shifts in consumer behavior:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Influence Over Traffic:</strong> The emphasis should be on influencing audiences where they are, even if it doesn't lead to immediate website traffic.</p></li><li><p><strong>Embrace Zero-Click Marketing:</strong> Accept that not all valuable interactions result in clicks; focus on brand presence and engagement within platforms.</p></li><li><p><strong>Adapt to Changing Buyer Journeys:</strong> Recognize that consumers' paths to purchase are non-linear and often occur without direct website interactions.</p></li></ul><p>By broadening strategies and focusing on where audiences truly engage, marketers can navigate the challenges of the current digital environment and achieve meaningful results.</p><hr><p><strong>#BrightonSEO #DigitalMarketing #RandFishkin #ZeroClickMarketing #SEOInsights</strong></p><hr><p>I had opportunity to meet SEO Sass companies: Wix DemandSphere , JetOctopus.com ( Virtually ), Nozzle , Screaming Frog, Ahrefs  ( Sorry I wasn't able to meet all )</p><p>All Sponsors - <a href=\"https://brightonseo.com/events/april-2025/sponsors\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">https://brightonseo.com/events/april-2025/sponsors</a></p><hr><p><a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/jordankoene/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Jordan Koene  from Previsible </a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/martha-van-berkel/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Martha van Berkel from Schema App </a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/michaelkingphilly/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Michael King from iPullRank, AIPRM </a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/tysonstockton/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Hosted by Tyson Stockton from Previsible</a></p><figure><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0262.webp\" alt=\"Three speaker headshots side by side, no captions\" width=\"1456\" height=\"1310\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0262\"></figure><p><strong>Navigating the Future of SEO: Insights from the Voice of Search Podcast at BrightonSEO San Diego 2024</strong></p><p>Attended the first live recording of the Voice of Search podcast at BrightonSEO San Diego 2024, hosted by Tyson Stockton (Co-Founder at Previsible). The panel featured industry experts:</p><p>The session delved into three main categories:</p><ol><li><p><strong>How SEO is Evolving</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Shifting Competitive Landscapes</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Essential Skill Sets for Future Success</strong></p></li></ol><hr><p><strong>Key Highlights and Takeaways:</strong></p><h3>1. The Future Dominance of Google</h3><h3>Michael King's Perspective: Google's Unassailable Position: </h3><ul><li><p>Believes Google will remain the fundamental search engine in the next five years due to its vast data and resources.</p></li><li><p><strong>Data Advantage</strong>: Google's 13 months of click data equals 17 years of Bing's data.</p></li></ul><h3>Jordan Koene's Counterpoint:</h3><ul><li><p><strong>Emerging Competition</strong>: Predicts a significant shift with competitors like OpenAI and verticalized search platforms challenging Google's dominance.</p></li><li><p><strong>Market Share Erosion</strong>: Highlights that Google is losing market share in specific areas like product search (e.g., Amazon holds 77% of product search in the U.S.).</p></li><li><p><strong>Verticalized Search Experiences</strong>: Anticipates growth in niche search tools catering to specific industries (e.g., healthcare, automotive).</p></li></ul><h3>Martha van Berkel's Insight:</h3><ul><li><p><strong>Fracturing of Search</strong>: Emphasizes that consumers will gravitate towards platforms offering the best experience, leading to a diversification beyond traditional search engines.<strong>API World Revival</strong>: Foresees a resurgence of API-driven experiences enhancing productivity and personalization.</p></li></ul><h3>2. The Impact of AI and Changing Tactics</h3><p><strong>AI's Role in Search:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Integration with Search</strong>: AI and large language models (LLMs) are influencing how search results are generated and presented.</p></li><li><p><a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/posts/venkata-pagadala_seo-ai-machinelearning-activity-7260789112796860416-t114\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><strong>What is RAG </strong></a><strong>(Retrieval Augmented Generation) and Importance to Understand- </strong>I made a video, check out and share your inputs </p></li><li><p><strong>Generative AI</strong>: Discussion on how generative AI might not replace traditional search but will augment it.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Shifting Tactics:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Structured Data Importance</strong>: Emphasis on the role of structured data and knowledge graphs in enhancing visibility, especially with AI-driven search results.</p></li><li><p><strong>Content Strategy Evolution</strong>: Need for unique, high-quality content that stands out in an AI-influenced ecosystem.</p></li></ul><p><strong>SEO Skillset Transformation:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Data Architecture Understanding</strong>: SEOs need to grasp data structures and relationships (e.g., knowledge graphs) to optimize for AI.</p></li><li><p><strong>Curiosity and Continuous Learning</strong>: Encouraged to ask 'why' and understand the business and technological reasons behind changes in the industry.</p></li><li><p><strong>Collaboration Across Departments</strong>: Working closely with content teams, data architects, and other stakeholders to create cohesive strategies.</p></li></ul><h3>3. Preparing for the Future Landscape</h3><p><strong>Adaptability is Key:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Embracing Change</strong>: SEOs must be open to evolving tactics and strategies in response to technological advancements.</p></li><li><p><strong>Expanding Roles</strong>: Future SEO roles may encompass AI management, data analysis, and more.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Strategic Thinking Over Tactics:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Asking the Right Questions</strong>: Move beyond checklists to understanding the broader business objectives and how SEO fits within them.</p></li><li><p><strong>Creativity and Innovation</strong>: Develop creative solutions rather than relying solely on traditional tactics.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Tools and Technologies:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>AI Integration</strong>: Leverage AI tools for content creation, data analysis, and optimization.</p></li><li><p><strong>Monitoring New Platforms</strong>: Stay informed about emerging search platforms and technologies (e.g., ChatGPT, Bing's integrations).</p></li></ul><h3>4. The Evolving Role of SEOs</h3><p><strong>From Tacticians to Strategists:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Business Alignment</strong>: SEOs should align their efforts with the company's strategic goals.</p></li><li><p><strong>Enhanced Communication Skills</strong>: Ability to articulate the value of SEO initiatives to executives and other departments.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Lifelong Learning:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Stay Informed</strong>: Keep up with industry changes, technological advancements, and evolving best practices.</p></li><li><p><strong>Skill Development</strong>: Invest in learning about AI, data science, and other relevant fields.</p></li></ul><h3>5. Audience Questions and Discussions</h3><p><strong>Blocking AI Crawlers:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Consensus</strong>: Generally not advisable to block AI from accessing site content, as it can limit visibility in AI-driven search results.</p></li><li><p><strong>Brand Visibility</strong>: Being present in AI responses is crucial for maintaining mindshare.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Measurement and Reporting:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Beyond Traditional Metrics</strong>: Incorporate social search, video search, and other platforms into SEO analysis as they become more relevant.</p></li><li><p><strong>Focus on Conversions</strong>: Ultimately, the impact on business outcomes is the most important metric.</p></li></ul><hr><p><strong>Final Thoughts</strong></p><p>The session highlighted the dynamic nature of SEO and the importance of adaptability. While Google's dominance is debated, the consensus is that the search landscape is diversifying due to AI and changing consumer behaviors.</p><p>SEOs are encouraged to:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Embrace Change</strong>: Be proactive in learning and adapting to new technologies.</p></li><li><p><strong>Think Strategically</strong>: Align SEO efforts with broader business goals and be innovative in approach.</p></li><li><p><strong>Develop New Skills</strong>: Focus on understanding AI, data structures, and enhancing soft skills like communication and strategic thinking.</p></li></ul><p>By staying informed and adaptable, SEOs can navigate the evolving landscape and continue to drive value in their organizations.</p><hr><p><strong>#BrightonSEO #SEO #AI #DigitalMarketing #VoiceOfSearch</strong></p><h2>Wil Reynolds from Seer Interactive</h2><p><a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/wilreynolds/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">https://www.linkedin.com/in/wilreynolds/</a></p><p><a href=\"https://info.seerinteractive.com/taking-action-from-digital-strategy-to-real-world-impact-2024\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">https://info.seerinteractive.com/taking-action-from-digital-strategy-to-real-world-impact-2024</a></p><figure><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0265.webp\" alt=\"LinkedIn profile header of Wil Reynolds, Seer Interactive (no photo)\" width=\"1488\" height=\"645\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0265\"></figure><h3>Embracing Change in SEO: Insights from Wil Reynolds at BrightonSEO San Diego 2024</h3><p>Attended a thought-provoking session by <strong>Wil Reynolds</strong>, founder of Seer Interactive, at BrightonSEO San Diego 2024. Wil challenged the traditional approaches to SEO, emphasizing the need for SEOs to evolve from mere tacticians to strategic thinkers who focus on providing answers and building genuine connections.</p><h3>Wil's Commitment to Social Causes</h3><p>In addition to his professional insights, Wil shared his personal commitment to social responsibility. He participates in an annual sleep out in Philadelphia to raise awareness and funds for homeless youth. He encouraged attendees to contribute to the cause, highlighting the importance of supporting those less fortunate.</p><h3>Key Themes and Takeaways:</h3><h3>1. The Imperative to Wake Up and Adapt</h3><h3>SEO as an Answers Business: </h3><ul><li><p>Wil reiterates that SEOs have always been in the business of providing answers, not just optimizing for search engines.</p></li><li><p>Emphasizes the need to pivot and focus on delivering genuine value to users.</p></li></ul><h3>Friction in Traditional Search:</h3><ul><li><p><strong>Inefficiencies in Search Experiences: </strong>Users face numerous obstacles like irrelevant results, ads, and poor user experiences on websites.Examples include difficulty finding specific car models or being bombarded with pop-ups and sign-up requests.</p></li></ul><ul><li><p><strong>Comparison with AI-Powered Answers:</strong>AI platforms like ChatGPT provide concise, direct answers without the clutter and friction of traditional search results.Encourages embracing AI as a tool to enhance user experience by reducing friction.</p></li></ul><h3>2. The Decline of Traditional SEO Practices</h3><p><strong>Over-Reliance on Checklists and Tactics:</strong></p><ul><li><p>SEOs have become complacent, focusing on minor optimizations rather than innovative strategies.</p></li><li><p>The \"sea of sameness\" where content and strategies are replicated without adding unique value.</p></li></ul><p><strong>The Google Monopoly's Impact:</strong></p><ul><li><p>Google's dominance has led to a lack of innovation and testing among SEOs.</p></li><li><p>Search results are cluttered with ads and irrelevant content, diminishing user trust.</p></li></ul><h3>3. The Rise of Social and Human-Centric Platforms</h3><p><strong>Humans Prefer Human Recommendations:</strong></p><ul><li><p>Platforms like LinkedIn, Reddit, and private messaging apps are growing because people value human interactions and recommendations.</p></li><li><p>Building relationships and community engagement are more effective than traditional link-building strategies.</p></li></ul><p><strong>The Importance of Building Fans:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Fans Over Traffic:</strong>Focusing on creating valuable content that resonates with the audience leads to loyal followers who trust and promote your brand.</p></li><li><p><strong>Distribution Through Relationships:</strong>Leveraging personal networks and genuine connections amplifies reach and influence.</p></li></ul><h3>4. Metrics That Matter</h3><p><strong>Re-evaluating Success Metrics:</strong></p><ul><li><p>Traditional metrics like organic traffic and rankings are less indicative of actual success.</p></li><li><p><strong>Quality Over Quantity:</strong>A smaller, engaged audience is more valuable than a large, unengaged one.</p></li><li><p><strong>Engagement and Conversions:</strong>Emphasizes tracking metrics like newsletter sign-ups, social shares, and direct engagement.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Case Studies and Personal Insights:</strong></p><ul><li><p>Shared examples where increased traffic did not correlate with increased engagement or revenue.</p></li><li><p>Highlighted how content that received fewer visits but higher engagement led to more meaningful outcomes.</p></li></ul><h3>5. Leveraging AI and Social Platforms Strategically</h3><p><strong>Embracing AI for Better User Experiences:</strong></p><ul><li><p>AI can provide more accurate, frictionless answers, improving user satisfaction.</p></li><li><p>SEOs should adapt by optimizing for AI-driven search and answer platforms.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Building Authority in AI Platforms:</strong></p><ul><li><p>Brands need to ensure they are accurately represented in AI-generated answers.</p></li><li><p>This involves creating high-quality, authoritative content that AI platforms recognize as valuable.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Using Social Platforms for Distribution:</strong></p><ul><li><p>Personal brands and employee advocacy can significantly enhance reach.</p></li><li><p>Company pages have limited impact compared to individual thought leaders within the organization.</p></li></ul><hr><h3>Actionable Strategies:</h3><ol><li><p><strong>Shift Focus from Traffic to Engagement: </strong>Prioritize content that fosters engagement and builds a loyal audience. Measure success through meaningful interactions rather than sheer volume.</p></li><li><p><strong>Invest in Building Personal Brands: </strong>Encourage team members to develop their personal brands on platforms like LinkedIn. Leverage these personal networks for greater content distribution and influence.</p></li><li><p><strong>Leverage AI Platforms: </strong>Optimize content for AI-driven search engines and answer platforms. Ensure your brand is accurately represented in AI-generated responses.</p></li><li><p><strong>Enhance Content Quality and Uniqueness: </strong>Move away from generic, keyword-stuffed content. Provide unique insights, expert opinions, and valuable answers that stand out.</p></li><li><p><strong>Engage in Social Listening and Community Participation: </strong>Actively participate in relevant online communities and discussions. Use these platforms to understand audience needs and tailor content accordingly.</p></li><li><p><strong>Test and Innovate: </strong>Return to the roots of SEO by experimenting with new strategies and testing outcomes. Be willing to take risks and learn from failures to drive innovation.</p></li></ol><hr><h3>Final Thoughts</h3><p>Will Reynolds advocates for a significant shift in how SEOs approach their work. The emphasis is on:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Human-Centric Strategies:</strong> Building genuine relationships, providing real value, and engaging authentically with audiences.</p></li><li><p><strong>Quality Over Quantity:</strong> Focusing on meaningful metrics that align with business goals rather than vanity metrics.</p></li><li><p><strong>Adapting to Technological Changes:</strong> Embracing AI and evolving platforms to stay ahead in the rapidly changing digital landscape.</p></li></ul><p>By refocusing efforts on these areas, SEOs can not only improve their effectiveness but also future-proof their strategies in an environment where traditional tactics are becoming less impactful.</p><hr><p><strong>#BrightonSEO #SEO #WilReynolds #DigitalMarketing #HumanCentricSEO</strong></p><h2>Philip Mastroianni from Homes.com </h2><figure><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0257.webp\" alt=\"LinkedIn profile header of Philip Mastroianni, SEO technical lead\" width=\"1488\" height=\"780\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0257\"></figure><p><strong>Unlocking Google's Indexing Secrets: Insights from Philip Mastroianni at BrightonSEO San Diego 2024</strong></p><p>Just attended Philip Mastroianni's enlightening session at BrightonSEO San Diego 2024, and it's a game-changer for anyone looking to improve their website's indexing and crawl budget. 🚀</p><p>Philip shared practical strategies on how he got his site indexed in about 90 days, emphasizing that simply building a website isn't enough - we need to optimize effectively to ensure Google can find and index our pages.</p><p><strong>Key Highlights and Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Solid Site Architecture</strong>: Establish a clear URL structure and hierarchy to help Google understand and navigate your site efficiently.</p></li><li><p><strong>Consistent URL Patterns</strong>: Use logical and consistent naming conventions across your site. For locations, decide on a format (e.g., \"California\" vs. \"CA\") and stick with it.</p></li><li><p><strong>Effective Internal Linking</strong>: More internal links to a page can significantly increase its crawl rate. Aim for quality over quantity - 50-60 well-placed links can boost crawling frequency.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Avoid Wasting Crawl Budget</strong>:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Redirects and Errors</strong>: Eliminate unnecessary redirects and fix error pages to prevent Google from wasting resources.</p></li><li><p><strong>Correct Canonical URLs</strong>: Ensure your canonical tags point to the correct pages to avoid confusing search engines.</p></li><li><p><strong>Minimize Crawl Traps</strong>: Remove or manage URL parameters that can lead Google into infinite loops.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Quality Content Matters</strong>:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Balance Length and Clarity</strong>: Longer content isn't always better. Focus on clear, concise information that matches user intent.</p></li><li><p><strong>Align Content with Hierarchy</strong>: Ensure your content fits within your site's structure - detailed content on product pages, broader topics on category pages.</p></li></ul><ul><li><p><strong>Optimize Site Speed</strong>: Faster sites get crawled more often. Monitor your site's response time and work on improving Core Web Vitals.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Utilize Sitemaps Effectively</strong>:</p><ul><li><p><strong>XML Sitemaps</strong>: Follow Google's guidelines - keep them under 50MB and 50,000 URLs, use accurate last modified dates, and organize them logically.</p></li><li><p><strong>HTML Sitemaps</strong>: Provide an alternate navigation path for users and search engines, aligning with your site's hierarchy.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Monitor and Analyze</strong>:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Server Logs</strong>: Regularly check your server logs to understand how Google crawls your site and identify any issues.</p></li><li><p><strong>Use the Right Tools</strong>: Leverage tools like Screaming Frog and Botify to visualize site structure and analyze crawling behavior.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Final Thoughts</strong></p><p>Optimizing for indexing and crawl budget is crucial, especially for large-scale websites. Philip's insights highlight the importance of a well-structured, fast, and user-friendly site that communicates effectively with search engines.</p><p>Let's take these strategies and apply them to our own projects to enhance visibility and performance on Google. </p><p><strong>#BrightonSEO #SEO #Indexing #CrawlBudget #DigitalMarketing</strong></p><h2>Ross Hudgens from Siege Media </h2><figure><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0260.webp\" alt=\"Ross Hudgens LinkedIn profile header with mutual connections line\" width=\"1488\" height=\"762\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0260\"></figure><p><strong>Maximizing Content Performance: Insights from Ross Hudgens at BrightonSEO San Diego 2024</strong></p><p>Attended Ross Hudgens' informative session at BrightonSEO San Diego 2024, where he shared valuable strategies for enhancing content marketing, particularly for mid to large-sized companies. 📈</p><p>With over 15 years in SEO and 12 years with Siege Media, Ross distilled his extensive experience into actionable advice covering content strategy, keyword research, content creation, and distribution.</p><hr><h3>1. Content Strategy and User Experience</h3><p><strong>Sticky Navigation with Call-to-Action (CTA):</strong></p><ul><li><p>Implement a sticky navigation bar with a contrasting CTA button to increase conversions.</p></li><li><p>Siege Media saw a <strong>39% increase in goal conversions</strong> after making this change.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Optimized Hero Images:</strong></p><ul><li><p>Transition from large hero images to smaller ones for faster load times and improved Core Web Vitals.</p></li><li><p>This change led to improved engagement metrics and a <strong>10% increase in organic traffic</strong>.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Enhanced Related Content Links:</strong></p><ul><li><p>Expand post-content related links from 3 to 6 (or more) to improve user engagement.</p></li><li><p>Ensure these links are highly relevant and curated to the content.</p></li><li><p>Resulted in increased time on site and a potential boost in organic traffic.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Internal Linking and Site Navigation:</strong></p><ul><li><p>Maintain consistent internal linking by including the blog or content section in the main navigation.</p></li><li><p>Aim for at least <strong>2% of internal links</strong> pointing to your content section to enhance visibility and performance.</p></li><li><p>Use direct links rather than hiding content in dropdown menus or footers.</p></li></ul><h3>2. Keyword Research and Audience Understanding</h3><p><strong>Audience-Centric Keyword Research:</strong></p><ul><li><p>Shift from relying solely on keyword tools to conducting audience research to identify relevant topics.</p></li><li><p>Focus on low-search-volume keywords that are highly relevant and less competitive.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Competitor and Comparison Content:</strong></p><ul><li><p>Create content that compares your products/services with competitors (e.g., \"Company A vs. Company B\").</p></li><li><p>Even with low search volumes, such content can drive significant qualified traffic and conversions.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Utilizing Organic Link Score:</strong></p><ul><li><p>Use metrics like Ahrefs' Keyword Difficulty to gauge the potential for earning organic links.</p></li><li><p>Target keywords with lower difficulty and higher link intent for better performance.</p></li></ul><h3>3. Content Creation and Optimization</h3><p><strong>First-Party Data Integration:</strong></p><ul><li><p>Leverage proprietary data in your content to provide unique value and insights.</p></li><li><p>Repurpose this data across multiple content pieces to enhance differentiation and authority.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Improved Content Introductions:</strong></p><ul><li><p>Use the APPB formula (Attention, Proof, Preview, Benefit) for compelling introductions.</p></li><li><p>This approach can increase reader engagement and time on page.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Visual Enhancements:</strong></p><ul><li><p>Incorporate custom images and multimedia elements where relevant.</p></li><li><p>Optimize images for SEO by targeting keywords that trigger image results in SERPs.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Content Templates and Formatting:</strong></p><ul><li><p>Utilize content templates that reflect the final web layout to streamline the content creation process.Ensure content is scannable with appropriate headings, bullet points, and concise paragraphs.</p></li></ul><h3>4. Content Distribution and Link Building</h3><p><strong>Strategic Outreach:</strong></p><ul><li><p>Move away from mass outreach tactics due to diminishing returns.</p></li><li><p>Focus on quality over quantity, targeting specific opportunities where outreach can make a significant impact.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Website Architecture for Distribution:</strong></p><ul><li><p>Feature important content prominently on your website to naturally attract traffic and links.</p></li><li><p>Use homepage features, top navigation, and highlighted sections to showcase valuable assets.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Leveraging Trends and Statistics:</strong></p><ul><li><p>Create and promote statistics or trends content, positioning your data prominently to attract backlinks.</p></li><li><p>Such content can become a reference point, earning organic links over time.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Final Thoughts</strong></p><p>Ross Hudgens emphasized the importance of continuous iteration and learning in content marketing. By focusing on user experience, strategic keyword targeting, high-quality content creation, and smart distribution methods, businesses can significantly enhance their content performance.</p><p>These strategies are especially crucial in today's competitive landscape, where understanding your audience and providing genuine value are key to standing out.</p><p>Let's apply these insights to drive better results in our own content marketing efforts!</p>","mastering-google-search-operators-comprehensive-guide":"<p>Introduction</p><p>The internet is a vast ecosystem of information, and Google is the gatekeeper. It's no surprise then, that understanding how to interact effectively with this powerful tool can vastly improve your research capabilities, SEO strategy, or just about anything you do online. This guide will deep-dive into Google's Advanced Search Operators, offering a more detailed and comprehensive understanding than ever before. Let's get started!</p><h3>Exact Match Search [\"\"]</h3><p>If you're seeking a specific sequence of words, enclose the phrase within double quotation marks. This command instructs Google to locate pages containing that exact sequence of words. For instance, [\"OpenAI GPT-4\"] will yield results that include this precise phrase.</p><h3>Exclude Words [-]</h3><p>By appending a hyphen (-) before a term, Google will filter out any results that include that term. For example, [Tesla -car] will return results about Nikola Tesla and the company, but exclude those related to the electric car.</p><h3>Site-Specific Search [site:]</h3><p>Need to search within a particular website? Use the [site:] operator followed by the URL. For instance, to search for articles about AI on the OpenAI website, you would input [site:openai.com AI].</p><h3>Related Sites [related:]</h3><p>Find websites similar to one you already like with the [related:] operator. Inputting [related:openai.com], for example, would yield a list of websites related to artificial intelligence and machine learning.</p><h3>A OR B [OR or |]</h3><p>If you're interested in finding pages that contain either of two search terms, you can use the OR operator. For instance, [OpenAI OR DeepMind] will return results containing either term.</p><h3>Wildcard Search [*]</h3><p>The asterisk (*) acts as a wildcard that Google will replace with any word(s) that make sense in the context. If you search for [OpenAI has * AI models], Google might show results including \"OpenAI has developed new AI models\".</p><h3>Search Within a Number Range [..]</h3><p>If you want to search within a specific numerical range, use two periods (..). For example, [laptop $500..$1000] will show results for laptops within that price range.</p><h3>File Type [filetype:]</h3><p>This operator is perfect for when you're searching for specific types of files, such as PDFs, PPTs, or XLS. Inputting [AI research filetype:pdf] will yield AI research papers in PDF format.</p><h3>Definitions [define:]</h3><p>To quickly find the definition of a word, use [define:] before the term. For example, [define: machine learning] will provide you with various definitions of machine learning.</p><h3>Cache [cache:]</h3><p>To see what a page looked like the last time Google visited it, use the [cache:] operator. For example, [cache:openai.com] will display the most recent cached version of the OpenAI website.</p><p>Search for Keywords in Title [intitle:], Page URL [inurl:], or Text Body [intext:]</p><p>These operators allow you to search for specific terms within the title, URL, or body of a webpage. For example, [intitle:\"machine learning\"] will return pages with \"machine learning\" in the title.</p><p>Finally thank you Billy Watts for your guidance.</p><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>Google's Advanced Search Operators are a powerful tool, allowing you to interact with the search engine on a whole new level. Whether you're an SEO expert or a curious internet user, understanding these operators will streamline your online searches, making them more effective and efficient. Happy searching!</p><p>This post is a beginner-friendly guide to Google's Advanced Search Operators. If you're interested in learning more about advanced SEO tactics or digital marketing strategies,</p>","simple-python-script-for-clustering-keywords":"<p>Simple Python Script for Clustering Keywords</p><p>📌Python code that performs clustering of keywords using the Agglomerative Clustering algorithm and TF-IDF vectorization. Here is a brief overview of the functions in the code:</p><p>📌 read_keywords(file_path) - reads the keywords from a CSV file specified by file_path and returns a list of keywords.</p><p>📌 write_clusters_to_csv(file_path, clusters, keywords) - writes the clusters of keywords to a CSV file specified by file_path. The clusters are assigned integer labels and are written to the second column of the output file, with the corresponding keyword in the first column.</p><p>📌 text_similarity(keywords) - calculates the TF-IDF similarity matrix of the input keywords and returns the matrix.</p><p>📌 cluster_keywords(similarity_matrix, num_clusters) - performs agglomerative clustering on the similarity matrix using num_clusters clusters and returns the cluster labels.</p><p>📌 main() - defines the input file, output file, and number of clusters, reads the keywords from the input file, calculates the similarity matrix, performs clustering, and writes the clusters to the output file.</p><p>Overall, this code can be used to cluster a set of keywords based on their similarity using TF-IDF vectorization and the Agglomerative Clustering algorithm, and write the resulting clusters to a CSV file.</p><p>Script Included </p><hr><pre>import cs\nimport numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.cluster import AgglomerativeClustering\nfrom sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer\n\n\n# Read keywords from input file\ndef read_keywords(file_path):\n    keywords = []\n    with open(file_path, \"r\") as f:\n        reader = csv.reader(f)\n        for row in reader:\n            keywords.append(row[0])\n    return keywords\n\n\n# Write clustered keywords to output file\ndef write_clusters_to_csv(file_path, clusters, keywords):\n    with open(file_path, \"w\", newline='') as f:\n        writer = csv.writer(f)\n        writer.writerow([\"Keyword\", \"Cluster\"])\n        for keyword, cluster in zip(keywords, clusters):\n            writer.writerow([keyword, cluster])\n\n\n# Calculate text similarity using TF-IDF\ndef text_similarity(keywords):\n    vectorizer = TfidfVectorizer()\n    keyword_matrix = vectorizer.fit_transform(keywords)\n    return keyword_matrix\n\n\n# Perform clustering\ndef cluster_keywords(similarity_matrix, num_clusters):\n    clustering = AgglomerativeClustering(n_clusters=num_clusters)\n    clusters = clustering.fit_predict(similarity_matrix.toarray())\n    return clusters\n\n\n# Main function\ndef main():\n    input_file = \"keywordsinput.csv\"\n    output_file = \"Cluster.csv\"\n    num_clusters = 5\n\n\n    keywords = read_keywords(input_file)\n    similarity_matrix = text_similarity(keywords)\n    clusters = cluster_keywords(similarity_matrix, num_clusters)\n    write_clusters_to_csv(output_file, clusters, keywords)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()v\n</pre><p>Input File </p><figure><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0134.webp\" alt=\"Longer keyword clustering table, keywords with cluster numbers\" width=\"852\" height=\"862\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0134\"></figure><p>Output </p><figure><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0135.webp\" alt=\"Keyword clustering table sorted by cluster\" width=\"844\" height=\"874\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0135\"></figure>","python-nlp-seo-basic-guide":"<p>Thanks to <a href=\"https://www.searchenginejournal.com/author/hamlet-batista/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Hamlet Batista</a>, <a href=\"https://www.jcchouinard.com/author/jean-christophe-chouinard/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Jean-Christophe Chouinard</a>, </p><h2>What is Python?</h2><ul><li>Python is an interpreted, object-oriented, high-level programming language with dynamic semantics</li><li>Programmers  &amp;  SEO fall in love with Python because of the <strong>increased productivity it provides</strong> like APIs integrations, and automating boring tasks using machine learning algorithms.</li></ul><h2>Why does Python matter to SEOs?</h2><p>Data Analysis from Multiple data sources</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.jcchouinard.com/get-number-of-indexed-pages-on-multiple-sites-with-python/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Get Number of Indexed Pages on Multiple Sites with Python</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.jcchouinard.com/rendering-on-large-scale-python/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Find Rendering Problems On Large Scale Using Python + Screaming Frog</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.jcchouinard.com/get-all-keywords-with-search-console-api/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Get All Your Search traffic With Google Search Console API (more than 50,000 keywords)</a></li></ul><p>Automation </p><ul><li>Handling large amounts of data, repeating tedious processes, merging tables, and importing data from different sources, are very difficult, and in many cases impossible to do manually.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.jcchouinard.com/backup-google-search-console-data-into-mysql-with-python/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Backup Google Search Console Data Into MySQL With Python</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.pemavor.com/build-automated-seo-audits-with-slackpython/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Build automated SEO Audits with Slack+Python</a></li></ul><h2><strong>How to learn Python</strong></h2><ul><li>Python official document provided us a guide - <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/</a></li><li>Free Basic python course - <a href=\"https://youtu.be/_uQrJ0TkZlc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Python Tutorial - Python Full Course for Beginners</a>  ( Highly Recommend to watch )</li></ul><h2>How to install environment </h2><ul><li>Step 1: <a href=\"https://www.anaconda.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Anaconda | The World's Most Popular Data Science Platform</a> </li><li>Step 2: After Installation, You can see a dashboard like below, Click Jupiter NoteBook</li></ul><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0111.webp\" alt=\"Anaconda Navigator home screen with application tiles\" width=\"2232\" height=\"1263\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0111\"><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0112.webp\" alt=\"Jupyter Notebook 6.4.5 launch tile in Anaconda Navigator\" width=\"496\" height=\"564\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0112\"><p>Step 3: Create a new file and start playing with it </p><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0114.webp\" alt=\"Jupyter file browser with New menu open\" width=\"2232\" height=\"1217\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0114\"><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0113.webp\" alt=\"Empty untitled Jupyter notebook\" width=\"2232\" height=\"447\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0113\"><h2>Example: </h2><h3><strong>Bulk Meta Description using NLP &amp; Python </strong></h3><ul><li>Reference Article - <a href=\"https://becominghuman.ai/text-summarization-in-5-steps-using-nltk-65b21e352b65?gi=84211a40a1ba\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">https://becominghuman.ai/text-summarization-in-5-steps-using-nltk-65b21e352b65?gi=84211a40a1ba</a> </li></ul><h3><strong>Reorganizing XML Sitemaps with Python for Fun &amp; Profit</strong></h3><ul><li>Reference Article -https://www.searchenginejournal.com/reorganizing-xml-sitemaps-python/295539/</li></ul><h2>NLP Tools</h2><ul><li>Sentiment -  Sentiment analysis (or opinion mining) is <strong>a natural language processing (NLP) technique used to determine whether data is positive, negative, or neutral</strong>. Sentiment analysis is often performed on textual data to help businesses monitor brand and product sentiment in customer feedback, and understand customer needs.  </li><li>Action Item - We can use it to check the heading tags or CTA </li><li>Entities - An entity can be <strong>any word or series of words that consistently refers to the same thing</strong>. Every detected entity is classified into a predetermined category</li><li>Salience score -In natural language processing, a salience score is always <strong>a prediction of what a human would consider being the most important entities in the same text</strong>. Several textual features contribute to the salience score</li><li>IBM Einstein - <a href=\"https://www.ibm.com/demos/live/natural-language-understanding/self-service/home\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">https://www.ibm.com/demos/live/natural-language-understanding/self-service/home</a> </li></ul><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0115.webp\" alt=\"IBM Watson NLU keyword extraction on rehab-program text\" width=\"2232\" height=\"1210\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0115\"><ul><li>How can we integrate with excel - <a href=\"https://seotoolsforexcel.com/watson-nl/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">https://seotoolsforexcel.com/watson-nl/</a>  </li></ul><p>Google NLP - <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/natural-language\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">https://cloud.google.com/natural-language</a> </p><ul><li><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/natural-language\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">https://cloud.google.com/natural-language</a> </li></ul><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0117.webp\" alt=\"Google Natural Language API demo analyzing sample text\" width=\"2232\" height=\"1255\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0117\"><p>Content Generator using NLP: <a href=\"https://rytr.me/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">https://rytr.me/</a> </p><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0116.webp\" alt=\"AI writing tool setup: &quot;addiction treatment in senior citizens&quot; blog section\" width=\"2232\" height=\"1306\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0116\"><h2>Custom Code</h2><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0118.webp\" alt=\"GPT-J-6B Gradio demo generating text on California rehab facilities\" width=\"1710\" height=\"1500\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0118\"><p><strong>#pythonseo</strong></p>","google-search-console-and-algorithm-update-september-2019":"<h3>3 Main Features from Google Console</h3><ol><li>Google Search Console Can Report on <strong>Same-Day Data </strong>( All data is based on the Pacific time zone ( PST )</li><li>Google Search Console New Feature : <strong>Structured Data Report for Breadcrumbs</strong></li><li><strong>Google Search Console APIs</strong>: Does they update to Date as Discover reports?</li></ol><p><strong>Bonus News: Google Algorithm Update on Sep 24 released is live from 10:56 AM ·</strong></p><h3>Update 1: Google Search Console Can Report on <strong>Same-Day Data </strong></h3><p><strong>NOTE: </strong>( All data is based on the Pacific time zone ( PST)</p><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0015.webp\" alt=\"Google Search Console Can Report on Same-Day Data ( All data is based on the Pacific time zone ( PST)\" width=\"940\" height=\"690\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0015\"><h3>Update 2: </h3><h3><strong>Google Search Console New Feature : Structured Data Report for Breadcrumbs</strong></h3><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0014.webp\" alt=\"Search Console sidebar menu: enhancements, manual actions, legacy tools\" width=\"289\" height=\"724\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0014\"><h3>Update 3: </h3><h3> Google Search Console APIs: Do they update to Date as Discover reports?</h3><h3>Q) What is Google Search Console APIs</h3><img src=\"{{media}}/f/0016.webp\" alt=\"Google Developers page &quot;What are the Search Console APIs?&quot;\" width=\"1488\" height=\"527\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-li-idx=\"0016\"><p>Q) Does they update to Date as Discover reports?</p><p>A) No, they are still 3 days behind</p><p>#seo #searchconsole #google #googlealgorithm #googletools</p>"};
